<section class="shell band faq" id="the-work-itself">
  <h2>The work itself</h2>

  <details>
    <summary>What kind of therapy does Reem practice?</summary>
    <div class="answer-body">
      <p>NARM for the patterns formed early in life, Somatic Experiencing for what the nervous
      system is still holding, and EMDR for specific traumatic material once there is enough
      stability. IFS-informed and Brainspotting elements are used where parts of a person
      disagree about the work.</p>
          <p><a href="/approach/">The four methods, in plain language</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>Do you offer sessions outdoors?</summary>
    <div class="answer-body">
      <p>Yes. Trail walk and movement sessions run one hour on Fridays in central
      Texas, for adults only, after a consult plus one to three online sessions. The fee is $125,
      paid in advance. <a href="/therapy/walk-and-talk/">Walk and talk therapy</a> covers
      how it works.</p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>Do you work with horses?</summary>
    <div class="answer-body">
      <p>Yes. Equine-assisted sessions are available near Dripping Springs, Texas, for clients,
      for LPC associates in supervision at $60 a session, and as optional training for
      therapists who want to work with horses themselves.</p>
          <p><a href="/therapy/equine/">Equine-assisted therapy near Dripping Springs</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </details>
</section>

<section class="band band--sand" id="understanding-the-methods">
  <div class="shell faq">
    <h2>Understanding the methods</h2>

    <details>
      <summary>What is somatic therapy?</summary>
      <div class="answer-body">
        <p>Somatic therapy is any approach that works with the body's sensations and responses
        rather than with thoughts alone. The working assumption is that stress and trauma leave
        the nervous system holding an unfinished response, and that noticing that response
        directly, in small doses, does what analyzing it cannot. In practice a session tracks
        sensation, breath, posture and the small movements a threat response never completed.
        Somatic Experiencing, developed by Dr Peter Levine, is the somatic method used here.</p>
            <p><a href="/approach/somatic-experiencing/">Somatic Experiencing in full</a>.</p>
    </div>
    </details>

    <details>
      <summary>Is EMDR a somatic therapy?</summary>
      <div class="answer-body">
        <p>Partly. EMDR is not usually classed as a somatic therapy, because its core mechanism is
        memory reprocessing rather than body tracking. But it attends closely to where a memory
        sits in the body and it pairs naturally with somatic work, which is why both are used in
        this practice rather than one or the other.</p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details>
      <summary>Is somatic therapy evidence based?</summary>
      <div class="answer-body">
        <p>The evidence base is real but smaller than for CBT or EMDR, and anyone who tells you
        it is either proven beyond question or pure invention is overstating their case.
        <a href="/approach/somatic-experiencing/">What the trials actually show</a>.</p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details>
      <summary>Is somatic therapy covered by insurance?</summary>
      <div class="answer-body">
        <p>Insurers reimburse psychotherapy sessions by billing code rather than by method, so a
        somatic session is billed as individual psychotherapy like any other. Blue Cross Blue
        Shield is accepted here. Under other plans sessions are self-pay at $240 for 50 minutes,
        and you may be able to claim out-of-network reimbursement.</p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details>
      <summary>Why is EMDR so controversial?</summary>
      <div class="answer-body">
        <p>The outcomes are not what is disputed. EMDR is recommended for PTSD by the World
        Health Organization and the American Psychological Association, and trials consistently
        show it helps. The argument is about why, not whether.
        <a href="/approach/emdr/">What the dismantling studies found</a>.</p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details>
      <summary>Is EMDR hypnosis?</summary>
      <div class="answer-body">
        <p>No. You stay awake, aware and in control throughout, you can stop at any point, and you
        remember the session afterwards. Nobody is put into a trance or given suggestions. The eye
        movements or taps are a dual-attention task, not an induction.</p>
            <p><a href="/approach/emdr/">What EMDR actually involves</a>.</p>
    </div>
    </details>

    <details>
      <summary>How long does EMDR therapy take?</summary>
      <div class="answer-body">
        <p>For a single recent incident, often six to twelve sessions. For developmental trauma
        laid down over years it is longer, because most of the work is in preparation and
        stabilization rather than in processing. Reem's emphasis is deliberately on phases one and
        two, since that is where stalled EMDR usually stalls.</p>
            <p><a href="/approach/emdr/">How EMDR runs here</a>.</p>
    </div>
    </details>

    <details>
      <summary>Can EMDR be done virtually?</summary>
      <div class="answer-body">
        <p>Yes, and it is the default here. Nothing essential to the protocol needs a shared
        room. {% include "partials/text/telehealth-texas.njk" %}
        <a href="/approach/emdr/">How EMDR runs over video</a>.</p>
      </div>
    </details>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="shell band faq" id="complex-ptsd">
  <h2>Complex PTSD</h2>

  <details>
    <summary>What are the 17 symptoms of complex PTSD?</summary>
    <div class="answer-body">
      <p>There is no official list of 17. That number comes from the DSM-IV criteria for PTSD,
      which counted 17 symptoms across re-experiencing, avoidance and arousal, and it has been
      recycled online as though it described complex PTSD. It does not. Complex PTSD is defined
      in the ICD-11 as the three PTSD clusters plus three further disturbances: difficulty
      regulating emotion, a persistently negative view of yourself, and difficulty sustaining
      relationships and feeling close to people. A checklist is not a diagnosis, and a
      recognizable pattern is worth bringing to an assessment rather than settling in your own
      head.</p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>What is the difference between PTSD and complex PTSD?</summary>
    <div class="answer-body">
      <p>PTSD usually follows an identifiable event or a small number of them. Complex PTSD
      follows prolonged, repeated exposure that was hard or impossible to escape, often early and
      often relational. The extra ground it covers is how you relate to yourself and to other
      people, not just how you respond to reminders of what happened.</p>
          <p><a href="/therapy/trauma/">Trauma and C-PTSD therapy here</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>Is complex PTSD in the DSM-5?</summary>
    <div class="answer-body">
      <p>No. The DSM-5 recognizes PTSD, including a dissociative subtype, but not complex PTSD
      as a separate diagnosis. The ICD-11, published by the World Health Organization, does.
      That difference matters mostly for paperwork: in the United States the clinical record and
      any insurance claim will usually say PTSD.</p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>Does EMDR work for complex PTSD?</summary>
    <div class="answer-body">
      <p>It can, with the preparation phases taken seriously. Applied straight to developmental
      trauma without that groundwork, standard EMDR often floods or flatlines, which is the most
      common reason people arrive here saying EMDR did not work for them. The IFS-informed
      version addresses exactly that.</p>
          <p><a href="/approach/ifs-informed-emdr/">Why standard EMDR stalls, and what changes</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>How many EMDR sessions for complex PTSD?</summary>
    <div class="answer-body">
      <p>More than the six to twelve typical of single-incident work, and the honest answer is
      that it depends on how much stabilization is needed first. Rather than committing to a
      number at the start, the arc is reviewed together as the work goes.</p>
          <p><a href="/approach/ifs-informed-emdr/">IFS-informed EMDR, and why preparation takes the time</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>Is complex PTSD a disability?</summary>
    <div class="answer-body">
      <p>It can be, depending on how much it limits daily functioning rather than on the label
      itself. Because complex PTSD is not a separate DSM-5 diagnosis, claims in the United States
      are generally filed under PTSD. Whether a particular claim succeeds is a question for a
      treating clinician and someone who handles disability claims, not for a website.</p>
    </div>
  </details>
</section>

<section class="band band--sand" id="for-therapists">
  <div class="shell faq">
    <h2>For therapists</h2>

    <details>
      <summary>How many supervised hours do I need for a Texas LPC?</summary>
      <div class="answer-body">
        <p>3,000 clock-hours under a Council-approved supervisor, at least 1,500 of them direct
        client counseling contact, over no fewer than 18 months, with a minimum of four
        supervision hours a month and no more than half of your total supervision hours in a
        group. The licensure guide on this site works through all of it, including the 60-month
        expiry on your Associate license.</p>
            <p><a href="/texas-lpc-licensure/">The Texas licensure guide, free and with every number</a>.</p>
    </div>
    </details>

    <details>
      <summary>Do you run retreats or training for therapists?</summary>
      <div class="answer-body">
        <p>Yes. An annual mindful trail retreat for interns, associates and licensed therapists,
        equine-assisted sessions and training near Dripping Springs, and periodic ethics
        continuing education for Texas LPCs. Associates in supervision with Reem are included in
        the annual retreat.</p>
            <p><a href="/for-therapists/retreats-training/">Retreats and equine training</a>.</p>
    </div>
    </details>


    <details>
      <summary>Are you accepting LPC Associates for supervision?</summary>
      <div class="answer-body">
        <p>Places are capped deliberately and open as associates finish, so ask about current
          availability. Supervision is $425 a month for three
          individual hours and one group hour, online and statewide in Texas.
        Applications start with a free 30-minute consult.</p>
            <p><a href="/for-therapists/lpc-supervision/">LPC supervision: the arrangement, the fee and how to apply</a>.</p>
    </div>
    </details>

    <details>
      <summary>Can my agency contract supervision for a short period?</summary>
      <div class="answer-body">
        <p>Yes. Three-month contracts are available for associates working within an agency or
        group practice at $500 a month.</p>
            <p><a href="/for-therapists/lpc-supervision/">Supervision terms in full</a>.</p>
    </div>
    </details>

    <details>
      <summary>Can you be my EMDR consultant?</summary>
      <div class="answer-body">
        <p>Reem is an EMDRIA Approved Consultant. Individual consultation is $185 an hour online,
        focused on IFS-informed EMDR for phases one and two, and it is free for her own LPC
        associates.</p>
            <p><a href="/for-therapists/emdr-consultation/">EMDR consultation toward certification</a>.</p>
    </div>
    </details>

    <details>
      <summary>Do you offer ethics CEUs?</summary>
      <div class="answer-body">
        <p>Ethics continuing education for Texas LPCs is offered periodically. Email the practice
        to ask about the next session.</p>
            <p><a href="/for-therapists/ethics-ceus/">Ethics CEUs on secondary trauma</a>.</p>
    </div>
    </details>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="shell band faq" id="safety">
  <h2>Safety</h2>

  <details>
    <summary>What if I am in crisis right now?</summary>
    <div class="answer-body">
      <p>This practice does not provide emergency care. Call 911 if you are in immediate
      danger, or call or text 988 for the Suicide and Crisis Lifeline. Reach out here once you are
      safe and we can talk about ongoing support.</p>
    </div>
  </details>
</section>

<section class="shell band" id="texas-notices">
  <h2>Texas notices</h2>
  <h3>Licensing and complaints</h3>
  <p>Reem Glasco holds Texas LPC license number 66562. To file a complaint about a Texas
  licensee, contact the Texas Behavioral Health Executive Council at 1-800-821-3205 or
  <a href="mailto:enforcement@bhec.texas.gov">enforcement@bhec.texas.gov</a> to request the
  appropriate form. Mailed complaints go to:</p>
  <p>Texas Behavioral Health Executive Council<br>
  Attn: Enforcement Division<br>
  George H. W. Bush State Office Building<br>
  1801 Congress Ave, Ste 7.300<br>
  Austin, TX 78701</p>
  <p>The Executive Council investigates and prosecutes professional misconduct by marriage and
  family therapists, professional counselors, psychologists, psychological associates, social
  workers and licensed specialists in school psychology. Not every complaint or dispute
  involves professional misconduct, and the Council will explain how to file either way.
  License verification is available at <a href="https://bhec.texas.gov">bhec.texas.gov</a>.</p>

  <h3>Access to your records</h3>
  <p>Under the Texas Medical Records Privacy Act (HB 4224, effective 1 September 2025) you have
  the right to access your health care records and to file complaints. Submit a written request
  to <a href="mailto:info@findingbalancecounselingpllc.com">info@findingbalancecounselingpllc.com</a>.
  Records are provided electronically within 15 business days unless you agree to another
  format or an exception applies.</p>

  <h3>Complaints about this practice</h3>
  <p>To raise a concern directly, email
  <a href="mailto:info@findingbalancecounselingpllc.com">info@findingbalancecounselingpllc.com</a>.
  To file a consumer complaint with the state, forms and instructions are on the licensing
  authority's website and, where applicable, the Texas Attorney General's site.</p>

</section>

